-% intro-v1
-% \section{Introduction}
-% \label{sec:introduction}
-
-% Federated learning enables multiple clients to collaboratively train a shared model while keeping their data distributed. To reduce communication costs, clients typically perform several local stochastic-gradient updates before sending their models to a central server for aggregation. However, when clients have different objectives, their local updates may drift in different directions, potentially affecting the convergence of the global model. \Scaf{} addresses this issue by introducing control variates to correct client drift \citep{karimireddy2020scaffold}.
-
-% Even when all clients optimize the same objective function, another challenge remains. With a constant step size, stochastic-gradient noise causes the global model parameters to fluctuate around the optimal solution. These fluctuations may persist after the algorithm reaches its stationary regime, where the expected global model parameters can differ from the minimizer of the objective. This systematic deviation is known as \emph{stationary mean bias}.
-
-% Increasing the number of clients reduces the stochastic variation in the gradients aggregated at the server. It is therefore natural to expect that averaging over more clients will also reduce stationary mean bias. However, the stochastic trajectories generated by multiple local updates can interact with the nonlinear structure of the objective function, producing bias components that do not necessarily vanish as the number of clients increases. This raises two closely related questions: Which components of stationary mean bias are reduced by increasing the number of clients, and how do different federated learning update mechanisms affect the remaining bias?
-
-% Understanding these questions is important for characterizing the long-run behavior of federated learning algorithms. In particular, it helps distinguish the effects of averaging stochastic gradients from those introduced by the local training and correction mechanisms used by different algorithms.
-
-% Recent work by \citet{mangold2025scaffold} investigates the stationary behavior of stochastic \Scaf{} under a constant step size. In the homogeneous setting, the existing analysis identifies a leading stationary mean-bias contribution proportional to $\gamma/N$, where $\gamma$ denotes the step size and $N$ is the number of clients. This contribution decreases as more clients participate. However, the explicit coefficient of the higher-order, client-independent $\gamma^2$ contribution has not been characterized at the resolution needed to distinguish the effects of local stochastic updates from those of persistent control variates.
-
-% To address these questions, we study \Scaf{} and \FedAvg{} under a common homogeneous setting. All clients participate in each communication round, optimize the same strongly convex and sufficiently smooth objective function $f$, and use stochastic gradients with independent bounded additive noise. By considering the same objective, noise assumptions, step size, client count, and number of local steps, we can directly compare the stationary behavior of the two algorithms and identify the contributions associated with their different update mechanisms.
-
-% Our analysis examines how stationary mean bias depends jointly on the step size $\gamma$ and the inverse client count $1/N$. Specifically, we characterize the long-run behavior of the global model together with the stochastic fluctuations of local models within each communication round. This allows us to derive explicit multidimensional stationary-bias expansions and distinguish contributions that decrease with the number of clients from those whose coefficients are independent of the client count.
